Do not send free-text form responses, medical information, injury details, claims information, employee case notes or raw page URLs to GA4 or advertising platforms.

## 6. Identity, matching and hashing discipline

### Permitted match parameters

Aim to supply at least six applicable customer-information parameters to Meta CAPI for lead events, subject to consent and data availability:

| Parameter | Collection rule | Transmission rule |
|---|---|---|
| `em` | Work email supplied in the enquiry | Lowercase, trim, then SHA-256 |
| `ph` | Optional phone supplied for follow-up | Normalise to E.164, then SHA-256 |
| `fn` | First name | Lowercase, trim, then SHA-256 |
| `ln` | Last name, if collected | Lowercase, trim, then SHA-256 |
| `ct` | Business/contact city, if legitimately collected | Lowercase, then SHA-256 |
| `st` | State, if legitimately collected | Lowercase, then SHA-256 |
| `zp` | Postcode, if legitimately collected | Lowercase, then SHA-256 |
| `external_id` | Pseudonymous CRM contact or lead identifier | SHA-256 under one documented convention |
| `fbp` | Meta browser identifier | Send un-hashed only where consent permits |
| `fbc` | Meta click identifier derived from a valid click | Send un-hashed only where consent permits |
| `client_ip_address` | Request metadata | CAPI transport only where approved; do not store longer than required |
| `client_user_agent` | Request metadata | CAPI transport only where approved |
| GCLID / GBRAID / WBRAID | Captured from auto-tagged Google visit | Store against the CRM lead and return through Google Data Manager |
| `event_id` | Unique conversion identifier | Same un-hashed ID in browser and server copies for deduplication |

Rules:

- Hash PII before it leaves the controlled server environment.

 succeeded in 0ms:
- Never send plain email, phone, name or address data to advertising platforms.
- Never double-hash values.
- Use the same normalisation convention across web, CRM, Google and Meta.
- Hashing is a matching control, not anonymisation and not permission to upload sensitive data.
- Do not place PII in URLs, data-layer keys visible to third parties, GA4 parameters, campaign names or event names.

## 7. Offline conversion loop

### CRM capture at lead creation

Each valid lead record should retain:

- Unique `lead_id` and original `event_id`
- Lead-entry event: Snapshot request, direct enquiry or call
- Landing page and campaign metadata using sanitised values
- GCLID, GBRAID, WBRAID, FBCLID-derived FBC, FBP and UTMs where consent permits
- First-touch and latest-touch timestamps
- Consulting service category
- Call-tracking ID where relevant
- Enquiry-processing consent state and timestamp
- Marketing-consent state and timestamp as a separate field
- CRM qualification, proposal and won timestamps
- Approved monetary value basis
- Upload status, upload timestamp and platform response

### Google Data Manager

- Configure Enhanced Conversions for Leads through Google Data Manager.
- Upload `qualified_call`, `lead_qualified`, `proposal_sent` and `closed_won`.
- Prefer upload latency under **24 hours**; **24–72 hours** is a warning state; more than **72 hours** fails the assigned methodology.
- Preserve the original click identifier and event time.
- Use a stable lead/order identifier to prevent duplicate imports.
- Keep GA4 equivalents observational where a native Google conversion action is used.
- Reconcile imported counts to CRM counts daily during launch and weekly thereafter.

### Meta CAPI

- Prefer CAPI Gateway for lower-maintenance server integration and matching support.
- Send website lead events through Pixel and CAPI using identical `event_name` and `event_id`.
- Send CRM stages through CAPI with an appropriate `action_source`; use `website` when the event is a continuation of a website-originated lead, and document any different source explicitly.
- Include `event_time`, `event_source_url` only after sanitisation, customer-information parameters, value and `currency=AUD`.
- Upload qualified and won changes within 24 hours where operationally possible.
- Suppress uploads when the required consent or legal basis is absent.
- Do not use the discontinued Meta Offline Conversions API workflow.

## 8. Server-side implementation and quality gates

Deploy sGTM on the first-party custom domain:

`tags.ergoworksconsulting.com.au`

Required controls:

- Web GTM forwards eligible GA4 and advertising events to sGTM; avoid an undocumented direct-to-platform fallback.
- Conversion Linker preserves GCLID, GBRAID and WBRAID.
- The server container validates hostname, event name, schema version, consent state and allowed parameters.
- Privacy transformations strip unapproved fields and sensitive query parameters.
- IP address and user-agent are forwarded only where required, consented and approved.
- Server logs retain only the minimum operational information and follow the approved retention schedule.
- Meta Pixel and CAPI use matching event IDs and timestamps within five minutes for duplicated browser/server events.

### Acceptance thresholds

| Measure | Pass | Warning | Fail / launch blocker |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| sGTM custom domain | Active | Configured, not active | Not configured |
| CAPI implementation | CAPI Gateway active | Manual CAPI | Pixel only |
| Meta EMQ for lead events | **≥8.0** | 6.0–7.9 | <6.0 |
| Browser/server deduplication | **≥90%** | 70–89% | <70% |
| Server/client hit ratio | **80–120%** | 50–79% or >120% pending duplicate diagnosis | <50% |
| Match-parameter completeness | **6+ applicable parameters** | 4–5 | <4 |
| Hash convention | Documented and verified | Documented, unverified | Inconsistent |
| Offline import latency | **<24h** | 24–72h | >72h |
| End-to-end validation | All canonical launch events pass | Partial coverage | A Primary event fails |

A server/client ratio over 100% can be legitimate when server capture recovers browser-blocked events, but the required operating band is 80–120%. Ratios above 120% require duplicate, retry and trigger inspection before optimisation.

## 9. Consent and Australian privacy controls

### Consent sequence

1. The CMP loads first and establishes default consent states.
2. Apply **Consent Mode v2, default denied, with no client- or server-side analytics/advertising transmission before consent**, including denied defaults for:
   - `analytics_storage`
   - `ad_storage`
   - `ad_user_data`
   - `ad_personalization`
3. No analytics, advertising, Pixel, CAPI, Enhanced Conversions, audience or call-tracking tag may transmit data before the required consent is recorded.
4. Consent updates propagate to both web GTM and sGTM.
5. The server container rejects advertising events that lack the required consent evidence.
6. Withdrawal of consent stops future processing and triggers the approved deletion/suppression workflow.

The assigned attribution methodology prefers an advanced-mode implementation. That conflicts with this campaign’s stricter requirement: **Consent Mode v2, default denied, with no client- or server-side analytics/advertising transmission before consent.** The stricter ErgoWorks rule governs. Cookieless pings may be considered only after explicit Australian legal/privacy approval. The methodology’s 700+ ad-clicks/day per country/domain modelling threshold is also not met by the verified last-30d Search volume of 407 clicks, so behavioural-modelling recovery must not be assumed.

### Separate consent purposes

- **Enquiry-processing consent:** permits Ergoworks to respond to the specific Snapshot request, direct enquiry or call. It must not be used as blanket marketing permission.
- **Marketing consent:** optional, unchecked and separately worded for newsletters, nurture email, SMS or audience use where applicable.
- Provide a clear privacy notice, platform disclosure, call-tracking disclosure, withdrawal mechanism and retention statement.

No ad form may request injury history, symptoms, diagnosis, treatment, workers’ compensation details, claim status or other health information.

## 10. Platform privacy allowlist and deny list

### Allowlist: data that may be sent

Only the following may be transmitted, after the applicable consent and privacy approval:

**Events**

- `view_service`
- `form_start`
- `snapshot_request`
- `direct_enquiry`
- `call_connected` without call content
- `qualified_call`
- `lead_qualified`
- `proposal_sent`
- `closed_won`
- `newsletter_signup` only where separate marketing consent exists

**Identifiers and matching data**

- SHA-256-normalised `em`, `ph`, `fn`, `ln`, `ct`, `st`, `zp` and `external_id`
- GCLID, GBRAID, WBRAID, FBP and FBC
- Unique `event_id`, `lead_id`, `call_id`, proposal ID and deal ID where pseudonymous and approved
- Client IP address and user agent only for the approved server-to-platform matching purpose
- Consent state and timestamp
- Sanitised campaign, source, medium and content identifiers

**Non-sensitive business parameters**

- Consulting service category
- Offer type
- Team-size band
- Timeframe band
- Qualification status using controlled values
- Call duration and non-sensitive disposition code
- Event timestamp
- Approved conversion value and `AUD` currency
- Schema version
- Sanitised page path without query strings or personal data

### Deny list: data that must never be sent

- Injury descriptions or history
- Pain, symptoms, diagnosis, disability or treatment information
- Workers’ compensation, insurance or claim details
- Medical or Physiotherapy appointment information
- Return-to-work case notes or employee health records
- Free-text enquiry content
- Call recordings, transcripts or summaries
- Names or details of affected employees
- Health, injury or claim classifications inferred from page or form behaviour
- Raw email, phone, name or postal address
- Sensitive URL parameters, query strings, fragments, form values or page titles
- Physiotherapy, occupational-health, ErgoEquip or Biosymm identifiers/events in the Consulting dataset
- Passwords, authentication tokens, financial-account information or government identifiers
- Any sensitive field merely transformed with SHA-256

Hashing does not make health, injury, claim or clinical data acceptable for platform transmission.

### Mandatory launch gate

Before any Pixel, CAPI, Enhanced Conversions, CRM audience or offline-upload workflow is activated, Australian legal/privacy review must approve:

- The complete CRM-to-platform field mapping
- Consent wording and CMP behaviour
- Enquiry versus marketing-consent separation
- Each permitted event and identifier
- Call tracking and any recording practices
- Data retention and deletion periods
- Server log contents and hosting/data-residency arrangements
- Google and Meta upload purposes
- Privacy-policy and collection-notice updates

Failure to obtain this approval blocks launch.

